            "<p>40024</p>"
          ],
          "correct": 0,
          "explanation": "<p>For multiplying numbers like&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>204</mn><mo>&times;</mo><mn>206</mn></math>, we can extend the base method to work with any three-digit numbers,&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Since these numbers are close to 200, let&rsquo;s use base 200 for this calculation:</p>\n<p>Identify deviations from the base (200):</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>204</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>206</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p>\n<p>Perform two calculations:</p>\n<p>Left part: 204 + 6 = 210</p>\n<p>Right Part:&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&times;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></math></p>\n<p>Combine the results:</p>\n<p>Left part is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>210</mn><mo>&times;</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>42000</mn></math> (multiply the base-related part)</p>\n<p>Add the right part: 42000 + 24 = 42024</p>"
        },
